05 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186308473 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166905 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186308474 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166905 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186308479 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166905 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186308480 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166905 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186308482 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166905 #edb/ref 1039 "Solving an Initial Value Problem for an Inhomogeneous System of Linear ODEs" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166905 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186166521 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166905 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "6d5ab3ac-770b-58c9-b30f-659773ea1a20" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166987 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186167825 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166987 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186167826 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166987 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186167827 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166987 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186167828 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166987 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186167829 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166987 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186167830 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166987 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186167831 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166987 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166987 #edb/ref 1045 "Solving Inhomogeneous Systems of ODEs" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166987 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186167825 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166987 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186050801 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166987 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "3c5e13b1-d0a0-579a-b4f6-fd82128b1d48" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167170 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304239 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167170 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304322 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167170 #edb/ref 1069 "q-329230" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167170 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167170 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the following inhomogeneous system of linear differential equations:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 4 & 0\\end{aligned}]x(t)+{e}^{-2t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe complementary solution to this system is given by\n\n$$\n{x}_{c}(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) \\\\ 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}],\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nThen, the general solution of the system is\n\n| $x(t)={x}_{c}(t)+$ {{field-1}} $\\cdot \\,\\,$ |  | $0$ |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-2}} |  |  |" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167170 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0ffa37f0-fb51-548b-8827-5b9bd6774f9c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167170 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that the general solution of an inhomogeneous system can be written as \n\n$$\nx(t)={x}_{c}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{c}(t)$ is the complementary solution (solution of the corresponding homogeneous system) and ${x}_{p}(t)$ is a particular solution.\n\nThe forcing term\n\n$$\n{e}^{-2t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nof the inhomogeneous system of ODEs is exponential. Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also exponential, i.e.\n\n$$\n{x}_{p}(t)={e}^{-2t}[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwhere $a,b$  are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the derivative of ${x}_{p}$ with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\n{x}_{p}^{′}(t)=-2{e}^{-2t}[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the unknown constants, we substitute ${x}_{p}$ and ${x}_{p}^{′}$ into our system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2{e}^{-2t}[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 4 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {e}^{-2t}[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]+{e}^{-2t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ -2[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 4 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-2a \\\\ -2b\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-b \\\\ 4a\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-2a \\\\ -2b\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-b+1 \\\\ 4a-2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding components, we get the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-b+1=-2a \\\\ 4a-2=-2b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, we get \n\n$$\nb=2a+1.\n$$\n\n \nSubstituting this into the second equation and solving for $b,$ we have\n\n$$\n4a-2=-2(2a+1)\\,⇒\\,a=0.\n$$\n\nSo, \n\n$$\nb=2(0)+1=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{x}_{p}(t)={e}^{-2t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167825 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186167826 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167825 #edb/ref 1061 587221 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167825 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "6c0c815f-38b0-5982-b2ff-0c436a438ef2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167825 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186167990 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167826 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186167830 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167826 #edb/ref 1061 587222 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167826 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "cef46dd3-49de-53b9-9dd8-4623716995ad" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167826 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186166902 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167827 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186167828 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167827 #edb/ref 1061 587225 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167827 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "316a7c1f-a4af-51c8-a7b8-c3ec021d1f6e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167827 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186166903 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167828 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186167829 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167828 #edb/ref 1061 587228 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167828 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "2c8cd38f-370b-5913-94e0-7f44f9fcda89" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167828 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186166904 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167829 #edb/ref 1061 587231 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167829 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "12093e8a-7d9d-5d68-80be-1242a4755c5d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167829 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186166905 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167830 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186167831 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167830 #edb/ref 1061 587234 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167830 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "a0524e0a-51fb-5d06-bc88-e401c42eb515" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167830 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186168018 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167831 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186167827 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167831 #edb/ref 1061 587235 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167831 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "ec433b4b-f6b5-5367-a7c3-7cb160d53783" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167831 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186168019 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167990 #edb/ref 1064 "Consider an **inhomogeneous** linear system of ordinary differential equations in component form:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{'}(t)={a}_{11}{x}_{1}(t)+{a}_{12}{x}_{2}(t)+{f}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{'}(t)={a}_{21}{x}_{1}(t)+{a}_{22}{x}_{2}(t)+{f}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we can package the unknown functions into vectors\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThen, the system can be written as\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nOften, the **forcing functions** $f_1(t), f_2(t)$ are linear combinations of a few basic functions (such as $e^{3t},$ $t^2,$ or $\\sin t$).\nIn that case, it is useful to write $\\mathbf{g}(t)$ as a sum of those basic functions times constant vectors.\n\nFor example, let's write down the system of linear ODEs below in matrix form.\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{'}(t)=4{x}_{1}(t)+2{x}_{2}(t)+8{e}^{3t} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{'}(t)=-3{x}_{1}(t)+5{x}_{2}(t)+10{e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, let's rewrite our system, highlighting the coefficients:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{'}(t)=4\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)+2\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)+{e}^{3t}\\cdot 8+{e}^{4t}\\cdot 0 \\\\ {x}_{2}^{'}(t)=(-3)\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)+5\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)+{e}^{3t}\\cdot 0+{e}^{4t}\\cdot 10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can write the system in matrix form:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 5\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nwhere $[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}]$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167990 #edb/ref 1065 14390 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167990 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "02676de3-99b6-59c3-bb19-fb44e8c8dda4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167990 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168018 #edb/ref 1064 "Recall that an inhomogeneous linear system has the form\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}'(t)=A\\mathbf{x}(t)+\\mathbf{g}(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant matrix and $\\mathbf{g}(t)$ is a known forcing term. The associated *homogeneous* system is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}'(t)=A\\mathbf{x}(t).\n$$\n\nA *general solution* of the *homogeneous* system is called the **complementary solution** and is denoted by $\\mathbf{x}_c(t)$.\nA **particular solution** is any single function $\\mathbf{x}_p(t)$ that satisfies the full inhomogeneous system.\n\nThese two pieces combine to give the *general solution* of the inhomogeneous system:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}(t)=\\mathbf{x}_c(t)+\\mathbf{x}_p(t)\n$$\n\nIndeed, notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}_{c}+{x}_{p})^{'} & ={x}_{c}^{'}+{x}_{p}^{'} \\\\  & =(A{x}_{c})+(A{x}_{p}+g(t)) \\\\  & =A{x}_{c}+A{x}_{p}+g(t) \\\\  & =A({x}_{c}+{x}_{p})+g(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $(\\mathbf{x}_c+\\mathbf{x}_p)' = A(\\mathbf{x}_c+\\mathbf{x}_p)+\\mathbf{g}(t)$, the sum is a solution of the inhomogeneous system.\n\nNotice that it doesn't matter which particular solution we take.\nSuppose we found two different particular solutions, $\\mathbf{x}_p(t)$ and $\\mathbf{y}_p(t).$ Let's examine their difference, $\\mathbf{w}(t) = \\mathbf{y}_p(t) - \\mathbf{x}_p(t)$.\n\nBy definition, we know that $\\mathbf{y}_p' = A\\mathbf{y}_p + \\mathbf{g}(t)$ and $\\mathbf{x}_p' = A\\mathbf{x}_p + \\mathbf{g}(t).$\nNow, let's find the derivative of the difference $\\mathbf{w}(t)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{w}^{'} & =({y}_{p}-{x}_{p})^{'} \\\\  & ={y}_{p}^{'}-{x}_{p}^{'} \\\\  & =(A{y}_{p}+g(t))-(A{x}_{p}+g(t)) \\\\  & =A{y}_{p}-A{x}_{p} \\\\  & =A({y}_{p}-{x}_{p}) \\\\  & =Aw\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe result $\\mathbf{w}' = A\\mathbf{w}$ means that the difference between any two particular solutions is itself a solution to the *homogeneous* system.\n\nThis is why *any* particular solution is enough. Any other particular solution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{y}_{p} = \\underbrace{(\\mathbf{y}_{p}-\\mathbf{x}_{p})}_{\\text{homogeneous}} \\:\\:\\: + \\underbrace{\\mathbf{x}_{p}}_{\\text{particular}} \\!\\!\\!\\!\\!.\n$$\n\nThe \"variety\" among particular solutions is already captured by the complementary solution $\\mathbf{x}_c(t)$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168018 #edb/ref 1065 14560 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168018 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "07b5883c-d1bc-5616-8d06-61b24dc79288" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168018 #edb/ref 1067 "Complementary vs. Particular Solutions" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168019 #edb/ref 1064 "To solve\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}'(t)=A\\mathbf{x}(t)+\\mathbf{g}(t),\n$$\n\nwe often look for a particular solution $\\mathbf{x}_p(t)$ whose *form matches the forcing term* $\\mathbf{g}(t)$.\n\nIn each case below, the unknowns are constant vectors (to be determined by substitution).\n\n- **Constant forcing.** If $\\mathbf{g}(t)$ is a constant vector, then we try\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}_p(t)=\\mathbf{a}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{a}$ is a constant vector.\n\n- **Polynomial forcing.** If $\\mathbf{g}(t)$ is a polynomial of degree $n$, then we try\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}_p(t) = \\mathbf{a}_n t^n+\\mathbf{a}_{n-1} t^{n-1}+\\cdots+\\mathbf{a}_1 t+\\mathbf{a}_0\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{a}_n,\\mathbf{a}_{n-1},\\ldots,\\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_0$ are constant vectors.\n\n- **Exponential forcing.** If $\\mathbf{g}(t)=e^{\\alpha t}\\mathbf{v}$ for some constant vector $\\mathbf{v},$ then we try\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}_p(t)=e^{\\alpha t}\\mathbf{a}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{a}$ is a constant vector.\n\n- **Trigonometric forcing.** If $\\mathbf{g}(t)=\\cos(\\beta t)\\mathbf{v}$ or $\\mathbf{g}(t)=\\sin(\\beta t)\\mathbf{v}$ for some constant vector $\\mathbf{v},$ then we try\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}_p(t)=\\cos(\\beta t)\\mathbf{a}+\\sin(\\beta t)\\mathbf{b}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ are constant vectors.\n\nIn the next step, we substitute the chosen $\\mathbf{x}_p(t)$ into the differential equation to solve for the unknown vectors.\n\nLet's see some concrete examples." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168019 #edb/ref 1065 14561 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168019 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "799084bf-9121-5217-8f53-70b9e09e61d2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168019 #edb/ref 1067 "Choosing the Form of a Particular Solution" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186302557 #edb/ref 1080 "3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186302557 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186302557 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0f405964-62e8-577d-8237-7ea766af47e6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186302592 #edb/ref 1080 "2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186302592 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186302592 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "14d92213-e5ed-56fb-8cb7-027b0073c730" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186302633 #edb/ref 1080 "{e}^{t}[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}\\end{aligned}]" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186302633 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186302633 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "1ab23c90-40c9-571e-9f79-118b3d83869d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186302635 #edb/ref 1080 "1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186302635 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 